  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1117 PM CDT MON AUG 3 2026\n\nHigh confidence exists in the upper-level pattern transition by\nThursday, as the low lifts northeast and allows heights to build.\nThe upper low over northern Georgia is beginning to get separated\nfrom the main trough as it lifts off to the northeast. Moisture\nwrapping around the low will maintain a 30-50% chance of showers\nand a few thunderstorms through Wednesday. By Thursday, the low\nwill begin to drift north, eventually being absorbed by another\napproaching upper level trough moving across the Ohio Valley. A\nmore typical summertime pattern sets up the weekend with weak\nflow aloft, leading to diurnally driven showers and thunderstorms\ninto early next week.\n\nBy Wednesday, we start to warm back up with highs rebounding into\nthe low to mid 90s. Humid conditions will keep max heat indices\nin the 95-100 degree range which will pose a moderate heat risk\nfor much of Central Alabama.\n\nLocalized visibility reductions to 1-3 miles are possible early\nWednesday morning, particularly in areas that received rainfall.\nThese visibility issues are expected to be brief and should have\nminimal to no impact on morning surface travel.\n\n16",
